+## Remarks
+
+The five-term diagram used in step 1.1 makes the five-lemma application visible. Write $D^k(X)=H^k_{\mathrm{dR}}(X)$, $S^k(X)=H_\infty^k(X;\mathbb R)$, $D^k(U,V)=D^k(U)\oplus D^k(V)$ and $S^k(U,V)=S^k(U)\oplus S^k(V)$. Here $W=U\cap V$, and $I_{U,V}=I_U\oplus I_V$ in the indicated degree.
+
+```tikzcd
+D^{q-1}(U,V) \arrow[r,"s_D"] \arrow[d,"I_{U,V}"'] & D^{q-1}(W) \arrow[r,"\Delta_D"] \arrow[d,"I_W"] & D^q(M) \arrow[r,"r_D"] \arrow[d,"I_M"] & D^q(U,V) \arrow[r,"s_D"] \arrow[d,"I_{U,V}"] & D^q(W) \arrow[d,"I_W"] \\
+S^{q-1}(U,V) \arrow[r,"s_S"'] & S^{q-1}(W) \arrow[r,"\Delta_S"'] & S^q(M) \arrow[r,"r_S"'] & S^q(U,V) \arrow[r,"s_S"'] & S^q(W)
+```
+
+Both rows are exact. In either row, $r$ is restriction to the ordered pair $(U,V)$, $s(a,b)=b|_W-a|_W$ is the $V-U$ difference, and $\Delta$ is the positive lift-differential connector. The two difference squares and the restriction square commute by the equalities in step 1.1; the connector square commutes by [F2], namely $I_M\Delta_D=\Delta_S I_W$. The four outside vertical arrows are isomorphisms by the hypotheses, giving the middle isomorphism by step 2.1. The diagram illustrates the argument; the proof above is independent of it.
